From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: A7om <hatter6822@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm: Add the Raspberry Pi 5 Model B machine

Add raspi5b, a board built on the BCM2712 SoC, with 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16 GiB
of RAM and the board revision code of the matching model.

It boots the way the Raspberry Pi firmware does. By default the guest
enters at EL2, and QEMU's PSCI over SMC stands in for the TF-A BL31 that
the firmware installs. With secure=on the guest owns EL3 and the GIC
Security Extensions instead, for secure firmware of its own. The
VideoCore keeps the top 4 MiB of the first GiB, which the device tree's
memory node leaves out. EL3 is entered by firmware given with -bios or
an ELF -kernel; a Linux Image given with -kernel still starts at EL2
with QEMU's PSCI, as the Linux boot protocol asks.

When no -dtb is given, the machine generates a device tree for the
modelled devices, with /chosen/stdout-path on UART10; builtin-dtb=off
passes none. A -dtb from the Raspberry Pi firmware is fixed up: nodes of
devices the model lacks are disabled, and /system/linux,revision gets
the board revision. The serial= property sets the serial number the
firmware reports.

The reset-status, reboot-flags and boot-count properties read what a
reset leaves for the boot it starts: the reset status, which holds the
partition the OS asked for, the firmware's reboot flags and the number
of boots so far. Given at startup, they make the first boot the one
that reset would have started, for a host that runs QEMU afresh for
each boot, reading the SD card afresh as the firmware does.
boot-partition names the partition of the card the boot files came
from, which /chosen/bootloader reports.

system_powerdown presses the power button on GIO 20 for 200 ms, which
Linux reports as KEY_POWER. The green activity LED on GIO AON 9 is an
LED device, so its changes show as trace events. A monitor's EDID
answers at 0x50 on HDMI0's DDC bus.

The SD card slot is on the first SD host, and -drive if=sd, the board's
default interface, puts a card in it. Without one the slot is empty, and
the monitor's change sd0 inserts a card later; the card is
/machine/sd-card for QMP. The slot's card-detect switch pulls GIO AON 5
low while a card is in. The generated tree describes the slot as the
firmware's tree does, with its card-detect GPIO and the regulators of
its supply and signalling voltage.

PCIe1's root port bus, pcie1.0, is the board's external connector. The
machine implies PCI_DEVICES, so QEMU's PCI Express devices and switches
can plug into it; the documentation's example puts an NVMe drive and an
igb network card behind a switch there.

pcie2-preinit=on starts PCIe2, RP1's link, as the firmware leaves it
with pciex4_reset=0 in config.txt, for bare-metal code that uses RP1
without setting up PCIe; by default it starts in reset, as the firmware
resets it. PCI address 0 is a valid BAR address on the board, since the
firmware puts RP1's peripherals there.

The stock Raspberry Pi OS kernel mounts its root file system from an SD
card on either device tree, or from an NVMe drive on PCIe1.
---
 hw/arm/Kconfig     | 9 +++++++++
 hw/arm/meson.build | 1 +
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/arm/Kconfig b/hw/arm/Kconfig
index 1f900fa..1794116 100644
--- a/hw/arm/Kconfig
+++ b/hw/arm/Kconfig
@@ -384,6 +384,15 @@ config RASPI
     select BCM2836_CONTROL
     select BCM2838_GPIO
 
+config RASPI5
+    bool
+    default y
+    depends on TCG && AARCH64
+    imply PCI_DEVICES
+    select BCM2712
+    select DDC
+    select LED
+
 config BCM2712
     bool
     select ARM_GIC
diff --git a/hw/arm/meson.build b/hw/arm/meson.build
index 00b030a..a381c31 100644
--- a/hw/arm/meson.build
+++ b/hw/arm/meson.build
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@ arm_common_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_RASPI', if_true: files(
   'raspi4b.c'
 ))
 arm_common_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_BCM2712', if_true: files('bcm2712.c'))
+arm_common_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_RASPI5', if_true: files('raspi5b.c'))
 arm_common_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_STM32F100_SOC', if_true: files('stm32f100_soc.c'))
 arm_common_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_STM32F205_SOC', if_true: files('stm32f205_soc.c'))
 arm_common_ss.add(when: 'CONFIG_STM32F405_SOC', if_true: files('stm32f405_soc.c'))
